<!--
    RE-friction round 2, need `simd-constant-string-initializer`, challenge
    653d88600f4238b24302b0ec (macOS x86-64 Crackme0x04), sub_100003790 at
    0x100003790  -  the 752-byte initializer that builds two consecutive
    character ranges and then spells the crackme's prompt and result messages
    out of them.  Verbatim bytes; the second chunk is the SSE constant table
    at 0x100003f30 the vectorised body indexes ({0,1,...,15}, {16 x 16},
    {32 x 16}).

    Option `retsplitglobal` (P8 goto-quality).  The initializer ends in one
    block that fills four message buffers  -  seventy-two byte stores  -  and
    returns, and three paths reach it: the `n < 0` (spelled out: n below zero) guard, the vectorised loop's
    exact-fit exit, and the scalar tail.

    ActionReturnSplit::isSplittable (blockaction.cc:2242) asks whether that is a
    BARE epilogue, cheap enough to give every predecessor its own private copy.
    It admits only MULTIEQUAL, COPY and RETURN and, for COPY, checks only that
    no INPUT is free  -  it never looks at the COPY's OUTPUT.  Upstream that is
    harmless, because the epilogue COPYs it was written for move registers; a
    COPY into a PERSISTENT Varnode is a store to a global, and it is what the C
    prints as `dat_ADDR = ...`.  So the block is cloned three times: 216
    statements, 46% of the function's text.  Both splitters share this one
    predicate, which is why `option returndup off` does not stop it  -  the
    goto-driven upstream action picks the block up instead.

    With the option on the block's observable global stores are counted and the
    split is declined past the bound (8), so the epilogue is emitted once.  A
    small global-writing epilogue still splits exactly as upstream: the bound,
    not a veto, is the design, matching `crossjumprevert`/`dedupitetail` (8
    printed ops) and `taildup` (12).

    Two passes over one function, `simdlane` left at its default in both so only
    the split moves.  Counts are over the whole transcript.
-->
